  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "9naNLcB3ry2X4EvXTktxhgMQKwc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:29.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Powerhouse provides the `OID` (Object ID) custom scalar type for unique identifiers in document models. Key patterns:\n\n- Objects in arrays must have an `id: OID!` field\n- IDs are non-deterministic, so they must be generated **before `dispatch`** with `generateId()` and passed in through the action input \u2014 never generated inside the reducer\n- `AddTodoItemInput` therefore carries `id: OID!` alongside `text`, and the reducer reads `action.input.id`\n- Update and delete operations require `id: OID!` to identify the target\n\n```typescript\n// In the editor - generate the ID before dispatching\nimport { generateId } from \"document-model\";\n\ndispatch(addTodoItem({ id: generateId(), text: input.text }));\n```\n\n```typescript\n// In the reducer - read the ID from the input\naddTodoItemOperation(state, action) {\n  state.items.push({ id: action.input.id, text: action.input.text, checked: false });\n}\n```\n\n`generateId()` is `crypto.randomUUID()` under the hood, so calling it inside a reducer breaks replay determinism \u2014 two peers replaying the same operation log would produce different IDs. Generating at the dispatch site gives you unique, consistent IDs *and* a reducer that stays a pure function of `(state, action)`.\n\n_Corrected 2026-08-13 against Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:29.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:29.000Z"
    }
  },
